{"causes":["The rise of existential philosophy in the early 20th century shaped Camus's perspective.","The devastation of World War II prompted a reevaluation of human existence and meaning.","Camus's personal experiences with absurdity and suffering informed his philosophical outlook."],"effects":["The essay catalyzed discussions on absurdism and existentialism in post-war Europe.","It influenced a generation of writers and philosophers, including Sartre and de Beauvoir.","The Myth of Sisyphus became a foundational text in modern philosophical education."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"existentialism","sourceName":"Existentialism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus","targetName":"The Myth of Sisyphus","context":"The philosophical movement of existentialism shaped the themes explored in The Myth of Sisyphus."},{"sourceSlug":"albert-camus","sourceName":"Albert Camus","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus","targetName":"The Myth of Sisyphus","context":"Albert Camus authored The Myth of Sisyphus in 1942, establishing a key work in existential philosophy."},{"sourceSlug":"world-war-ii","sourceName":"World War II","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus","targetName":"The Myth of Sisyphus","context":"The writing of The Myth of Sisyphus took place during the tumultuous period of World War II."},{"sourceSlug":"absurdism","sourceName":"Absurdism","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus","targetName":"The Myth of Sisyphus","context":"The Myth of Sisyphus is a defining text of the philosophy of absurdism, articulating its core principles."},{"sourceSlug":"greek-mythology","sourceName":"Greek Mythology","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus","targetName":"The Myth of Sisyphus","context":"The essay contains allegorical references to Sisyphus, a character from Greek mythology."}],"places":[{"name":"Algiers, Algeria","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Major influence"},{"name":"Montpellier, France","role":"Philosophical development"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Myth of Sisyphus","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-myth-of-sisyphus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1125903","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Myth_of_Sisyphus"],"tags":["literary work","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"'One must imagine Sisyphus happy.' — Albert Camus, The Myth of Sisyphus (1942)","legacySummary":"The Myth of Sisyphus contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Myth of Sisyphus is a 1942 philosophical essay by Albert Camus (1913–1960) that stands as one of the foundational texts of Absurdist philosophy. The essay opens with Camus's audacious claim that 'there is but one truly serious philosophical problem, and that is suicide' — arguing that the fundamental question for philosophy is not metaphysical but existential: why should one continue to live given the irrationality and meaninglessness of existence? Camus defines the absurd as the tension between humanity's deep desire for clarity, meaning, and order, and the universe's silent, indifferent refusal to satisfy that desire.

The essay develops through a tripartite structure: first, an analysis of the 'absurd feeling' as a philosophical starting point; second, a critique of three philosophical responses to the absurd — 'philosophical suicide' (Kierkegaard's leap of faith, Husserl's phenomenological return to essence), which Camus rejects as intellectual dishonesty; and physical suicide, which he also rejects; and finally, 'absurd revolt' — the affirmation of life in full consciousness of its meaninglessness. The essay's most celebrated section is the closing meditation on Sisyphus, the Greek hero condemned by the gods to roll a boulder up a hill for eternity only to watch it roll back down. Camus declares that Sisyphus must be imagined happy — his revolt against the absurdity of his fate, his full consciousness of it, and his continued effort constitute a human triumph.

The Myth of Sisyphus was published in the same year as Camus's novel The Stranger (1942) and closely associated with Jean-Paul Sartre's Being and Nothingness (1943) and the post-World War II existentialist movement in Paris — though Camus later rejected the existentialist label, insisting that Absurdism was distinct from existentialism (which, he argued, evaded the absurd through commitment or religion). The phrase 'one must imagine Sisyphus happy' has become one of the most quoted philosophical conclusions of the 20th century, and the essay's concepts — the absurd, revolt, freedom, passion — entered the general cultural vocabulary of the postwar world, influencing writers from Samuel Beckett to Haruki Murakami.","historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Myth of Sisyphus significantly shaped existential philosophy and influenced post-war thought, prompting individuals to confront the absurdity of existence and find meaning in their struggles.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
